dequate control of finish-rolling temperature can contribute to the formation of coarse polygonal ferrite. In low Nb content, major hardening is due to grain refinement, and grain-refining elements enhance strength. Nb-free steel is used as a comparison for toughness improvement when finish rolled at lower temperatures. [Data: Entities (67, 949, 908, 113); Relationships (61, 1976, 1867, 122)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Hardening and transition temperature relate to cleavage stress\",\n      \"explanation\": \"HARDENING encompasses grain refinement, solid solution, precipitation, and transformation mechanisms, and transition temperature is determined by hardening due to precipitation, dislocations, and related mechanisms. Very low transition temperatures are a specific case of transition temperature. Toughness is represented in terms of the normal stress acting on the cleavage surface, specifically the {100} plane. The α-matrix can itself be toughened, and fine Nb(C,N) precipitates form in the α-matrix during cooling. [Data: Entities (959, 1204, 674, 126); Relationships (2610, 2614, 1324, 1325, 127)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Alternative refinement routes include second-phase dispersion and matrix toughening\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Grain refinement is attained by fine dispersion of a second phase, and fine dispersion of a second phase is a way to attain grain refinement. Low Nb content also has major hardening due to grain refinement. Beyond grain refinement, TOUGHENING refers to improvement in toughness achieved by toughening the α-matrix itself, and toughening improves toughness. A mixed structure of acicular plus fine-grained polygonal ferrite provides significant improvement in toughness. [Data: Entities (887, 904, 949, 903, 1056); Relationships (1776, 1854, 1852, 2257)].\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}, "metrics": {"attempted_request_count": 1, "successful_response_count": 0, "failed_response_count": 0, "responses_with_tokens": 1, "prompt_toke